1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,140
They've been called the near-perfect food and the miracle crop.

2
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:15,100
From the mighty soybean in all its forms to the world's most widely consumed legume.

3
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:17,680
That's enough to feed about two million people.

4
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:23,420
They supply a plentiful source of protein, and they also produce gas in more ways than one.

5
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:25,560
That's why our beans are so much better.

6
00:00:25,561 --> 00:00:29,560
Now, we're spilling them. Beans on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Beans.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,820
The world produces nearly 500 billion pounds of them a year.

9
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:56,920
That's an impressive 70 pounds for every man, woman, and child on Earth.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,120
They're one of the world's oldest crops, a source of cholesterol-free protein, rich

11
00:01:07,121 --> 00:01:14,040
in fiber and complex carbohydrates, low in fat, and packed with vitamins and minerals.

12
00:01:14,041 --> 00:01:17,900
Hundreds of bean varieties grow around the globe.

13
00:01:17,901 --> 00:01:24,220
The number one bean grown in the U.S. is also our most versatile, the soybean.

14
00:01:24,221 --> 00:01:31,680
American soybean growers annually harvest nearly 75 million acres of the bean, or around 180 billion pounds.

15
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:41,700
Like other beans, soybeans come from a family of vegetables called legumes and are harvested for the seeds, or beans, inside the pods.

16
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:47,160
Sure, you can turn soy into soy sauce and tofu.

17
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:50,280
But soy foods don't stop there.

18
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:52,240
How about hot dogs and burgers?

19
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:55,180
Want cheese with that? More soy.

20
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:58,300
Don't like burgers? How about chicken or meatballs?

21
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:01,460
And for dessert, soy ice cream.

22
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,320
Still, much of the soybean's value lies in the oil it produces.

23
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:13,789
Surprisingly, almost 80% of all edible fats and oils consumed in the U.S.

24
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:15,280
come from soybean oil.

25
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:17,860
This is soybean salad oil.

26
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:21,980
You find it in salad dressings, mayonnaises, frying oils.

27
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:25,780
This is an example of hydrogenated soybean oil.

28
00:02:25,781 --> 00:02:28,980
You would find this in a number of applications,

29
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:33,880
including shortenings, non-dairy coffee creamers, cookies.

30
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:38,140
If you put these two oils together, margarine.

31
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:42,040
Before that oil ends in the frying pan or in your Twinkie,

32
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,400
it must first be extracted from the soybean.

33
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:51,940
The CHS oil seed processing plants in Fairmont and Mankato, Minnesota,

34
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:57,000
operate around the clock to process more than 5 billion pounds of soybeans each

35
00:02:57,001 --> 00:03:01,680
year, which results in 1 billion pounds of soybean oil.

36
00:03:01,681 --> 00:03:05,800
Our trucks are able to come in and deliver the soybeans directly from the

37
00:03:05,801 --> 00:03:10,520
fields 24 hours a day, seven days a week. We average 300 truckloads of soybeans

38
00:03:10,521 --> 00:03:12,480
delivered per day.

39
00:03:12,481 --> 00:03:17,240
Soybeans ready for processing start at the top of this five-story soybean crushing

40
00:03:17,241 --> 00:03:19,240
facility.

41
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:25,000
The beans are continuously fed into the top of this machine where a series of screens

42
00:03:25,001 --> 00:03:28,000
located at different levels do the separating.

43
00:03:28,001 --> 00:03:33,000
This is where the field material is removed from the mainstream of the beans.

44
00:03:33,001 --> 00:03:39,320
After they pass out through this machine, they're partially clean.

45
00:03:39,321 --> 00:03:44,040
On the fourth floor, machines quickly heat the soybeans to 170 degrees Fahrenheit.

46
00:03:44,041 --> 00:03:48,040
Right now the beans are in their whole form.

47
00:03:48,041 --> 00:03:52,040
They're very hot. They're 170 degrees.

48
00:03:52,041 --> 00:03:56,040
The reason why this is done is to provide an easier separation between

49
00:03:56,041 --> 00:04:00,040
the outer covering or the hull of the bean from the inner portions of the bean.

50
00:04:00,041 --> 00:04:04,040
This separation allows the hull of the soybean to be

51
00:04:04,041 --> 00:04:08,040
easily removed when the soybeans reach the grinders on the third floor.

52
00:04:08,041 --> 00:04:12,040
Here's an example of what's coming through this

53
00:04:12,041 --> 00:04:12,741
machine.

54
00:04:12,742 --> 00:04:19,040
Here you see the soybean pieces with some of the holes that have been removed.

55
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:26,280
One level down, large rotating cylinders referred to as flaking mills flatten the crust soybeans

56
00:04:26,281 --> 00:04:30,040
into dime-sized flakes to increase their surface area.

57
00:04:30,041 --> 00:04:37,040
Increasing the surface area allows the greatest percentage of the soybean oil to be extracted

58
00:04:37,041 --> 00:04:44,040
You can physically grab these flakes and feel the oil as it's being pressed through the surface.

59
00:04:44,041 --> 00:04:48,040
The flattened flakes now head for extraction.

60
00:04:48,041 --> 00:04:56,040
The solvent, hexane, washes over the flakes, chemically bonds with the oil, and separates it from the flakes.

61
00:04:56,041 --> 00:05:01,040
The process is repeated multiple times to extract the greatest amount of oil.

62
00:05:01,041 --> 00:05:06,040
After extraction, heat evaporates the hexane from the soybean oil.

63
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:09,720
There's plenty of value left in the flakes after the oil is gone.

64
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:15,500
CHS grinds over 80% of the now defatted flakes into soybean meal,

65
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:18,300
widely used in the animal feed industry.

66
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:21,920
But the soybean oil isn't ready to eat yet.

67
00:05:22,940 --> 00:05:26,440
Every day, semis transport over 1.5 million pounds of it

68
00:05:26,441 --> 00:05:30,260
to the CHS plant in Mankato, 50 miles away.

69
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:36,440
We receive all the crude oil that is processed at Fairmont, Minnesota,

70
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:42,200
and we receive approximately 36 truckloads a day of their production oil.

71
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:45,040
The crude oil is unloaded in all these tanks,

72
00:05:45,540 --> 00:05:50,060
and we can store roughly 7 million pounds of raw crude oil in our plant.

73
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:54,580
The plant pumps crude soybean oil through a secondary refining process,

74
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:57,420
which washes and removes impurities from the oil,

75
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:00,980
converting it into a form suitable for consumption.

76
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:06,680
Right now we're loading finished soybean oil into a truck,

77
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:12,460
We load over 3 million pounds a day into trucks and rail cars for delivery throughout the world.

78
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:16,980
Two additional soybean commodities are produced by CHS.

79
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:20,300
Lecithin is a byproduct of the refining process.

80
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:22,320
This is lecithin.

81
00:06:22,321 --> 00:06:27,680
Its most common use is as an emulsifier, which means it just holds things together.

82
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:32,700
You'll find it in cosmetics, cooking sprays, and read your candy bar wrapper.

83
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:34,680
You're going to probably see it there.

84
00:06:34,681 --> 00:06:37,900
The other is soy flour

85
00:06:37,901 --> 00:06:41,180
If you go to a grocery store right now and you buy bread

86
00:06:41,181 --> 00:06:45,360
most of the bread in the United States has defatted soy flour in it

87
00:06:45,361 --> 00:06:49,420
Made from the remaining defatted flakes and ground into a powder

88
00:06:49,421 --> 00:06:54,840
Soy flour acts as a protein source and natural preservative in foods

89
00:06:54,841 --> 00:07:00,740
including pancake mixes, pastas, donuts, breads, and pet food

90
00:07:00,741 --> 00:07:05,740
Vegetarian and vegan markets also use soy flour as a source of protein.

91
00:07:05,741 --> 00:07:10,740
The soy flour gets produced into a product called textured soy protein.

92
00:07:10,741 --> 00:07:16,740
And that is used in a lot of applications where you can replace meat.

93
00:07:16,741 --> 00:07:20,740
While the creation of soy-based meat substitutes is relatively new,

94
00:07:20,741 --> 00:07:27,740
the soybean itself has been feeding families and farm animals since its cultivation began in China,

95
00:07:27,741 --> 00:07:29,940
more than 3,000 years ago.

96
00:07:31,380 --> 00:07:34,480
One of the earliest references of soybean use in the U.S.

97
00:07:34,780 --> 00:07:36,220
comes from the Civil War.

98
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,440
When coffee beans were scarce,

99
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,660
soldiers ground up dried soybeans,

100
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:43,360
referred to as coffee berries instead.

101
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:49,220
Thank American chemist George Washington Carver

102
00:07:49,221 --> 00:07:51,520
for first identifying many of soybeans' uses.

103
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:55,260
Known for his research with the peanut,

104
00:07:55,261 --> 00:07:58,510
Carver also experimented with the soybean.

105
00:07:58,511 --> 00:08:01,760
From mayonnaise to paint, Carver developed

106
00:08:01,761 --> 00:08:06,478
over 100 edible and industrial uses from the soybean.

107
00:08:06,479 --> 00:08:09,860
Long before Carver, the Chinese ground

108
00:08:09,861 --> 00:08:13,319
up soybeans and mixed them with water to create soy milk.

109
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:15,200
It's been around in China since

110
00:08:15,201 --> 00:08:17,384
at least the first century AD.

111
00:08:17,385 --> 00:08:21,460
But soy milk production in the US didn't start until the

112
00:08:21,461 --> 00:08:23,004
early 20th century.

113
00:08:23,005 --> 00:08:29,260
In the 1990s, a large number of lactose intolerant people, and others wanting

114
00:08:29,261 --> 00:08:34,820
to get away from dairy products, helped soy milk jump from natural food stores to supermarket

115
00:08:34,821 --> 00:08:41,660
shelves. Today, nearly 35% of the entire soy food market goes to soy milk.

116
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:48,081
One company enjoying soy milk's rise in popularity is Zen Soy.

117
00:08:48,082 --> 00:08:50,340
The New Jersey-based company blends

118
00:08:50,341 --> 00:08:54,780
and packages soy milk products with a 12-month shelf life,

119
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:58,540
refrigerated soy milk with a 90-100 day shelf life,

120
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:00,760
and a line of soy puddings.

121
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:05,360
Creating the soy base starts with whole soybeans and hot water.

122
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:08,480
The mixture is ground into soy slurry.

123
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:12,760
The slurry enters a high-speed centrifuge,

124
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:15,960
which filters out the okara, or fiber.

125
00:09:15,961 --> 00:09:22,960
The soy liquid then receives an injection of high temperature steam to decontaminate it.

126
00:09:22,961 --> 00:09:29,960
It's then pumped inside a vacuum vessel, where it's flash cooled to remove undesired bean-like flavors.

127
00:09:29,961 --> 00:09:35,960
This is where we receive our liquid bulk shipments of soy base.

128
00:09:35,961 --> 00:09:44,960
In the course of a 24-hour period, we can receive 1.1 million pounds of soy base, which equates to 130,000 gallons.

129
00:09:44,961 --> 00:09:47,200
From here, it goes to our blending area.

130
00:09:48,620 --> 00:09:54,360
In the blending room, Zen Soy first adds ingredients to approximate the look and taste of cow's milk.

131
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:59,380
Today's batch includes cocoa powder for Zen Soy's chocolate soy milk.

132
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:06,940
After pasteurization, achieving soy milk's extended shelf life comes down to container sterilization.

133
00:10:06,941 --> 00:10:14,560
Today we're packaging the Zen Soy into a refrigerated extended shelf life half gallon

134
00:10:14,561 --> 00:10:20,620
container where we can achieve 90 to 100 days of refrigerated shelf life compared to two

135
00:10:20,621 --> 00:10:22,480
weeks on a standard milk filler.

136
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:29,960
The filler sterilizes each container with hydrogen peroxide and a dose of ultraviolet

137
00:10:29,961 --> 00:10:30,661
light.

138
00:10:30,662 --> 00:10:38,760
Then a blast of 285 degree heat evaporates the hydrogen peroxide solution before each

139
00:10:38,761 --> 00:10:41,397
carton is filled with soy milk.

140
00:10:41,398 --> 00:10:46,160
This specific filler can produce 9,000 units an hour. On

141
00:10:46,161 --> 00:10:52,142
a typical 24-hour run, that's 90,000 gallons of soy milk.

142
00:10:52,143 --> 00:10:55,080
That's just part of the more

143
00:10:55,081 --> 00:11:00,840
than 200 million gallons of soy beverages Americans consume each year.

144
00:11:00,841 --> 00:11:05,260
Research suggests that soy milk and other soy foods can lower the risk of heart disease,

145
00:11:05,261 --> 00:11:08,320
certain cancers, and osteoporosis.

146
00:11:08,321 --> 00:11:13,660
But other research indicates too much soy can be harmful.

147
00:11:13,661 --> 00:11:19,460
Soybeans contain a class of phytoestrogens called isoflavones, an estrogen-like chemical

148
00:11:19,461 --> 00:11:21,780
found in plants.

149
00:11:21,781 --> 00:11:28,120
Studies suggest eating too much soy can reduce the effects of naturally produced estrogen

150
00:11:28,121 --> 00:11:34,540
and cause menstrual irregularity in women and may lower the sperm count in men.

151
00:11:34,541 --> 00:11:41,900
Of course, soybeans offer a lot more than sustenance, from biofuels, adhesives, inks

152
00:11:41,901 --> 00:11:48,540
and crayons to insulation, lubricants, foams and plastics.

153
00:11:48,541 --> 00:11:53,240
It's no wonder the soybean has been called the miracle crop.

154
00:11:53,241 --> 00:11:57,640
Both carver and auto industrialist Henry Ford recognized the soybean as a viable source

155
00:11:57,641 --> 00:12:00,860
for plant-based plastics and biofuel.

156
00:12:00,861 --> 00:12:07,360
In 1941, Ford attached a soy-based plastic trunk lid to his car and demonstrated its

157
00:12:07,361 --> 00:12:11,780
durability for reporters with an axe.

158
00:12:11,781 --> 00:12:20,780
62 years later, in 2003, agricultural equipment maker Case IH began replacing exterior steel

159
00:12:20,781 --> 00:12:25,780
panels on their 18-ton combines with a soybean-based plastic composite.

160
00:12:25,781 --> 00:12:31,460
Essentially what we're providing with the soybean-based panels is a complete circle.

161
00:12:31,461 --> 00:12:37,820
We take the crop out of the field, it goes through a manufacturing process, it becomes

162
00:12:37,821 --> 00:12:43,340
a trim panel for one of our Case IH combines and then that Case IH combine is back in the

163
00:12:43,341 --> 00:12:45,576
field harvesting the same crop again.

164
00:12:45,577 --> 00:12:48,780
We're utilizing one of the very important commodities

165
00:12:48,781 --> 00:12:51,999
that our customers grow and produce.

166
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,560
Although only a 3% savings in weight over its steel

167
00:12:56,561 --> 00:13:02,920
equivalent, the formability and cost of soybean-based plastic panels make them an ideal alternative

168
00:13:02,921 --> 00:13:04,600
for companies like Case IH.

169
00:13:05,540 --> 00:13:08,880
The molding process is much more precise

170
00:13:08,881 --> 00:13:12,560
than what we can reproduce with steel manufacturing.

171
00:13:12,561 --> 00:13:15,020
The molds utilized to manufacture

172
00:13:15,021 --> 00:13:18,100
the soybean-based composite panels

173
00:13:18,101 --> 00:13:20,400
are much less costly

174
00:13:20,401 --> 00:13:23,540
than if we were doing a tooling system

175
00:13:23,541 --> 00:13:25,620
for steel components

176
00:13:25,621 --> 00:13:28,180
of comparable configuration and shape.

177
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:31,480
The farmers who drive these red behemoths

178
00:13:31,481 --> 00:13:32,540
season after season

179
00:13:32,541 --> 00:13:37,140
Harvest after harvest, what a ruggedness comparable to steel.

180
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:43,000
One of the advantages of the Case IH trim panels is certainly durability.

181
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:48,960
I'd like to demonstrate that for you just as Henry Ford did some time ago.

182
00:13:54,260 --> 00:14:00,280
As you can see where the bowling ball did hit, there's no physical damage to the trim panel.

183
00:14:00,900 --> 00:14:03,920
But soybean use doesn't stop at the plastic panels.

184
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:06,660
This bean can also provide power.

185
00:14:07,340 --> 00:14:13,960
Not only are K-Sai's combines produced with soy-based paneling for the exterior of the combine,

186
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:18,260
they're also certified for the use of soy-based biofuels.

187
00:14:19,180 --> 00:14:23,000
Today's soy biodiesel is one of the fastest-growing renewable fuels,

188
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:26,760
with 600 million gallons produced in 2008.

189
00:14:26,761 --> 00:14:30,420
Soybeans aren't the only beans on the bush.

190
00:14:31,160 --> 00:14:33,480
There's another class called the dry bean.

191
00:14:33,980 --> 00:14:35,900
Chances are you've eaten your share.

192
00:14:38,140 --> 00:14:43,360
It takes just one acre of land to produce 356 pounds of soybean protein.

193
00:14:44,440 --> 00:14:48,820
It takes 18 acres of land to produce the same amount of beef protein.

194
00:14:50,100 --> 00:14:53,660
Beans will return on Modern Marvels, here on History.

195
00:14:56,760 --> 00:15:06,100
In the universe of beans, soy may be the king of versatility, becoming foods and non-food products that belie its true nature.

196
00:15:07,180 --> 00:15:10,460
But there's another type of bean that most often stays a bean.

197
00:15:11,180 --> 00:15:17,080
It ends up on our salads, in our soups and chilies, and alongside our hot dogs, the dry bean.

198
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:22,720
According to the U.S. Dry Bean Council, there are 14 major dry bean classes.

199
00:15:22,721 --> 00:15:28,820
Like other legumes, beans have the ability to pull nitrogen from the air and transport

200
00:15:28,821 --> 00:15:36,880
it to the soil, making them ideal for rotation with nutrient-depleting crops.

201
00:15:36,881 --> 00:15:41,820
For centuries, cultures around the world have valued dry beans not only for their low cost

202
00:15:41,821 --> 00:15:49,480
and nutritional content, but because they're edible even after long periods of storage.

203
00:15:49,481 --> 00:15:54,005
also made them ideal rations for sailors spending months at sea.

204
00:15:54,006 --> 00:15:56,480
Since the mid-1800s, the prevalence

205
00:15:57,380 --> 00:15:59,925
of this small white bean on U.S.

206
00:15:59,926 --> 00:16:04,380
warships earned it an additional moniker, the Navy bean.

207
00:16:04,381 --> 00:16:11,380
Today, they're most commonly found in cans of baked beans.

208
00:16:11,860 --> 00:16:17,460
Since the 1920s, this brick building positioned beside Casco Bay in Portland, Maine, has been

209
00:16:17,461 --> 00:16:21,220
producing B&M baked beans with ingredients and implements

210
00:16:21,221 --> 00:16:25,220
they consider to be part of a New England tradition.

211
00:16:25,221 --> 00:16:28,660
Sweet molasses, 200-pound steel pots,

212
00:16:28,661 --> 00:16:31,540
and fiery red brick ovens.

213
00:16:31,541 --> 00:16:34,580
They started with seven of these brick ovens

214
00:16:34,581 --> 00:16:38,760
in the early 20s, and then it grew to 14, and then 28.

215
00:16:38,761 --> 00:16:41,700
And currently, we have 76 brick ovens

216
00:16:41,701 --> 00:16:42,840
that we bake our beans in.

217
00:16:42,841 --> 00:16:45,140
And it's done very much the same way

218
00:16:45,141 --> 00:16:46,780
it was done when they originally started.

219
00:16:46,781 --> 00:16:49,220
the process has never changed.

220
00:16:49,221 --> 00:16:52,320
Turning the small white beans into golden brown nuggets

221
00:16:52,321 --> 00:16:55,880
begins with a wash through the riffle machine.

222
00:16:55,881 --> 00:16:59,000
In these riffles, we take out any foreign material,

223
00:16:59,001 --> 00:17:01,080
whether it's rocks, corn, or anything

224
00:17:01,081 --> 00:17:04,380
will get caught in these riffles.

225
00:17:04,381 --> 00:17:07,980
Then it drops into this tank where they're pumped over

226
00:17:07,981 --> 00:17:09,900
into the blancher.

227
00:17:09,901 --> 00:17:12,100
Rather than boiling the beans, the blancher

228
00:17:12,101 --> 00:17:14,040
acts like a giant steamer.

229
00:17:14,041 --> 00:17:17,040
Inside, the temperature reaches 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

230
00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:20,040
The purpose of this is to blanch the beans,

231
00:17:20,041 --> 00:17:22,040
so the beans can take in moisture,

232
00:17:22,041 --> 00:17:24,040
take in more water,

233
00:17:24,041 --> 00:17:27,040
and so once they come out of this steamer,

234
00:17:27,041 --> 00:17:28,040
they can go downstairs,

235
00:17:28,041 --> 00:17:31,040
and they'll make it a lot easier for the molasses

236
00:17:31,041 --> 00:17:34,040
to be absorbed into the beans in the next pot of the process.

237
00:17:35,040 --> 00:17:38,040
One floor down, workers load up each pot

238
00:17:38,041 --> 00:17:41,040
with 250 pounds of blanched beans,

239
00:17:41,041 --> 00:17:43,040
then position them under the sauce station.

240
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:49,460
Over here is your sauce, which is made out of molasses and sugar and water.

241
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:52,400
And then over here, you'll have a mustard slurry come out.

242
00:17:53,100 --> 00:17:54,680
And this is pork slurry.

243
00:17:58,200 --> 00:18:02,100
And we wait for the liquid to all drain, and then we give it a good stirrer.

244
00:18:02,660 --> 00:18:06,240
I do anywhere from 100 to 130 of these a day.

245
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:12,380
Soaked with sauce, each pot of beans heads to one of B&M's 76 brick ovens.

246
00:18:12,381 --> 00:18:19,120
Timing is crucial to the baked beans taste and determining the right amount of heat is all about the bubble.

247
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:25,040
These ovens are old enough so that they have no thermostat on them.

248
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:30,420
The only way we can tell if it's the right temperature is to watch the bubble on the outside of the pot.

249
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:32,380
And we adjust the burners to that.

250
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:34,300
There's a nice little bubble all the way around.

251
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:37,380
That's how we know we have the right amount of heat in these ovens.

252
00:18:37,860 --> 00:18:39,340
This is old world technology.

253
00:18:39,480 --> 00:18:40,420
This is hands on.

254
00:18:40,421 --> 00:18:45,060
We're true to the old-fashioned New England baked beans.

255
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:48,140
We still do it the way they did it 150 years ago.

256
00:18:48,680 --> 00:18:50,260
Nice steam coming up.

257
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:51,380
Oh, you can tell.

258
00:18:51,620 --> 00:18:53,560
Those beans right there are wicked good.

259
00:18:54,540 --> 00:18:56,080
Once baking is complete,

260
00:18:56,460 --> 00:18:58,780
each pot of beans is hung over a metal trough

261
00:18:58,781 --> 00:19:00,040
and drained of sauce.

262
00:19:02,240 --> 00:19:04,480
Workers dump the pots down a steel chute,

263
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:06,620
which leads the beans to the canning room.

264
00:19:07,440 --> 00:19:09,860
Here, the drained sauce is added back to the beans.

265
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:12,980
as each can passes under the sauce waterfall station.

266
00:19:15,020 --> 00:19:18,880
There, they get a second dose of sauce to fill up the can

267
00:19:18,881 --> 00:19:20,860
and give it that distinctive New England flavor.

268
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:24,640
That's just an integral part of D&M beans.

269
00:19:28,100 --> 00:19:29,960
During a typical day here,

270
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:32,340
we'll do somewhere in the neighborhood of 9,000 cases,

271
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:35,360
which is about 115,000 individual cans.

272
00:19:37,100 --> 00:19:39,760
The secret ingredient in our product is time.

273
00:19:39,761 --> 00:19:46,280
You have to give the bean time to absorb the sugars and molasses flavors before you can.

274
00:19:48,360 --> 00:19:55,120
The beans baked at B&M are just part of the 2.5 billion pounds of dry beans American farmers produce each year.

275
00:19:56,220 --> 00:19:59,720
In the United States, about 40 states grow dry beans.

276
00:20:00,300 --> 00:20:04,360
But only about 18 states grow them for commercial operations.

277
00:20:04,361 --> 00:20:11,360
The top bean producing state is North Dakota and they grow about 42% of the beans grown in the U.S.

278
00:20:11,361 --> 00:20:14,350
The number one dry bean grown in the U.S.

279
00:20:14,351 --> 00:20:18,360
is the pinto, occupying over 600,000 acres of farmland.

280
00:20:18,361 --> 00:20:23,360
That's about 1.1 billion pounds of beans.

281
00:20:23,361 --> 00:20:31,360
The pinto bean, along with an assortment of dry beans we consume today, originated in South and Central American regions.

282
00:20:31,361 --> 00:20:34,540
Some were domesticated over 7,000 years ago.

283
00:20:35,420 --> 00:20:40,040
As the cultivation of these beans spread throughout North America, so did the methods for growing them.

284
00:20:41,600 --> 00:20:46,920
Native American tribes taught early settlers a planting technique they called the Three Sisters.

285
00:20:48,060 --> 00:20:50,500
Beans were grown in combination with corn and squash.

286
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:55,220
The corn provided stalks for the beans to climb.

287
00:20:55,221 --> 00:21:01,180
The prickly squash leaves deterred animals and provided shade to keep the soil moist

288
00:21:01,181 --> 00:21:03,980
and prevent weed growth.

289
00:21:03,981 --> 00:21:07,460
The bean plant supplied nitrogen to replenish the soil.

290
00:21:07,461 --> 00:21:12,940
Of course, commercial beans aren't grown that way today.

291
00:21:12,941 --> 00:21:17,140
Many dry beans pass through facilities like the Kelly Bean Company, headquartered in western

292
00:21:17,141 --> 00:21:18,880
Nebraska.

293
00:21:18,881 --> 00:21:24,460
The family owned and operated company has been in the bean business since 1927.

294
00:21:24,461 --> 00:21:26,460
amassed one big hill of beans.

295
00:21:28,760 --> 00:21:31,860
Through our multiple plants in the western half of the United States,

296
00:21:31,861 --> 00:21:35,360
we originated about 12 different varieties of dry beans.

297
00:21:35,361 --> 00:21:38,760
So, whether you're going to find pinto beans in your taco,

298
00:21:38,761 --> 00:21:40,460
or great northern beans in your soup,

299
00:21:40,461 --> 00:21:42,760
or navy beans in your can of baked beans,

300
00:21:42,761 --> 00:21:44,460
we handle all varieties of beans.

301
00:21:44,461 --> 00:21:45,960
Through all of our operations,

302
00:21:45,961 --> 00:21:49,660
we're running 250 million pounds of beans per year.

303
00:21:49,661 --> 00:21:57,920
At Kelly Beans Mitchell, Nebraska facility, 80,000 pounds of dry beans pass through the

304
00:21:57,921 --> 00:22:04,280
cleaning and sorting equipment every day.

305
00:22:04,281 --> 00:22:09,540
The beans first pass through a series of screens to shake out farmland debris and beans that

306
00:22:09,541 --> 00:22:12,260
don't size up.

307
00:22:12,261 --> 00:22:17,180
Next, gravity decides which beans make it to the customer.

308
00:22:17,181 --> 00:22:24,180
Here we are at the end of the gravity machine, the weight machine, and what you can see is

309
00:22:24,181 --> 00:22:29,180
the lighter product that's coming off the deck is being separated from the good beans.

310
00:22:29,181 --> 00:22:31,180
This will go into livestock beans.

311
00:22:31,181 --> 00:22:35,180
The heavy product here will go into the bag.

312
00:22:35,181 --> 00:22:38,180
It is the finished product.

313
00:22:38,181 --> 00:22:43,180
All those dry beans in the middle enter the high-tech side of Kelly Bean's operation,

314
00:22:43,181 --> 00:22:45,180
the electric eyes.

315
00:22:45,181 --> 00:22:51,180
These speedy bean counters employ eight chutes, each handling ten lanes of bean traffic.

316
00:22:51,181 --> 00:22:56,180
Their job? Search for and pluck out the bad beans based on color.

317
00:22:56,181 --> 00:23:01,180
Today, the eyes are set up for white beans like the Great Northern.

318
00:23:01,181 --> 00:23:05,180
What this machine does is it separates the white beans.

319
00:23:05,181 --> 00:23:12,180
It takes the bad beans out so that the product can be a good bean when it goes to the consumer.

320
00:23:12,181 --> 00:23:18,020
I can run anywhere from 10,000 pounds to 12,000 pounds of beans per hour.

321
00:23:20,720 --> 00:23:27,120
And right here, you can see on the eye, there's the camera, that little square shot there,

322
00:23:27,600 --> 00:23:32,980
so that this machine can read the bean, read the color of the bean.

323
00:23:34,040 --> 00:23:38,880
As each bean passes in front of an electric eye, it's compared against a white background.

324
00:23:39,700 --> 00:23:41,960
I think we're about ready to fire it up.

325
00:23:42,180 --> 00:23:53,180
It's looking for blight, worm tube, frozen bean, and also a rust color on the bean.

326
00:23:53,181 --> 00:24:01,180
It reads that bean, it's only got a split second to get it out of there.

327
00:24:01,181 --> 00:24:06,180
Compressed air blows out any questionable beans within the first 30 lanes.

328
00:24:06,181 --> 00:24:11,180
These beans go back to the top of the machine and make another pass through the next 30 lanes

329
00:24:11,181 --> 00:24:13,280
to ensure none of the good beans were missed.

330
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:17,640
Any rejects in these lanes get one final pass

331
00:24:17,641 --> 00:24:18,600
through the last 20.

332
00:24:20,680 --> 00:24:22,080
The main thing of it is,

333
00:24:22,081 --> 00:24:25,400
what we're trying to do is get a good product.

334
00:24:25,401 --> 00:24:27,980
That's what goes to the customer, right there.

335
00:24:29,380 --> 00:24:31,800
Each one ton tote of good beans ends up

336
00:24:31,801 --> 00:24:34,340
at Kelly's 400,000 square foot packaging

337
00:24:34,341 --> 00:24:37,020
and storage facility in Morrill, Nebraska.

338
00:24:37,021 --> 00:24:42,580
We're going to package about 200,000 pounds of beans today.

339
00:24:43,400 --> 00:24:45,620
That's enough to feed about 2 million people.

340
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:51,960
Kelly's beans end up on grocery store shelves in the food service industry and are exported

341
00:24:51,961 --> 00:24:53,280
to more than 20 countries.

342
00:24:55,540 --> 00:25:01,260
Even though dry beans claim the number one bean eaten in America with the pinto, it still

343
00:25:01,261 --> 00:25:03,420
isn't the most widely consumed bean in the world.

344
00:25:04,500 --> 00:25:05,200
What is it?

345
00:25:05,201 --> 00:25:07,200
Here's a hint.

346
00:25:10,580 --> 00:25:15,060
The British devour 500 million cans of baked beans every year,

347
00:25:15,480 --> 00:25:17,660
making them the world's largest consumer.

348
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:21,440
That's 16 cans of baked beans per bread per year.

349
00:25:22,320 --> 00:25:24,620
Beans will return on Modern Marvels.

350
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:30,000
In Italy, it's called the ceci.

351
00:25:30,880 --> 00:25:33,340
The Spanish named it the garbanzo.

352
00:25:33,341 --> 00:25:37,340
Other cultures know it as the chickpea.

353
00:25:37,341 --> 00:25:44,340
Whatever you call it, more than 18.5 billion pounds of this bean produced a year make it

354
00:25:44,341 --> 00:25:48,340
the most widely consumed legume in the world.

355
00:25:48,341 --> 00:25:53,340
Over 65% of the planet's chickpeas come from India.

356
00:25:53,341 --> 00:25:59,340
But it's their use in hummus, a popular dish originating from Middle Eastern and Mediterranean cultures,

357
00:25:59,341 --> 00:26:04,340
cultures that contribute to the chickpeas' global dominance.

358
00:26:04,341 --> 00:26:08,340
Hummus is conquering America, too, where it's commonly eaten as a

359
00:26:08,341 --> 00:26:11,340
condiment or dip.

360
00:26:11,341 --> 00:26:15,340
Tribe Mediterranean Foods has seen its hummus sales skyrocket,

361
00:26:15,341 --> 00:26:19,340
thanks to a more health-conscious and diverse population and

362
00:26:19,341 --> 00:26:22,340
large vegetarian following.

363
00:26:22,341 --> 00:26:26,340
Tribe Mediterranean started producing hummus back in 1993,

364
00:26:26,341 --> 00:26:29,400
But the hummus category was very, very tiny in the whole US.

365
00:26:29,401 --> 00:26:32,280
Across the country, we probably did maybe $5 million

366
00:26:32,281 --> 00:26:34,020
in total retail sales.

367
00:26:34,021 --> 00:26:37,520
Today's number is well over $180 million in retail sales.

368
00:26:37,521 --> 00:26:40,220
So the escalation of the popularity of the product

369
00:26:40,221 --> 00:26:43,620
has grown tremendously in the past several years.

370
00:26:43,621 --> 00:26:46,500
In order to turn the 200,000 pounds of chickpeas

371
00:26:46,501 --> 00:26:49,460
it receives every week into hummus,

372
00:26:49,461 --> 00:26:53,340
Tribe must first give the dry beans a good soaking,

373
00:26:53,341 --> 00:26:55,540
10 tons at a time.

374
00:26:55,541 --> 00:26:57,920
In this area, we hydrate and clean the pea.

375
00:26:57,921 --> 00:27:01,600
We have one silo that we hydrate the peas overnight.

376
00:27:01,980 --> 00:27:05,420
The other silo is for processed peas during the day.

377
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:08,760
We'll hydrate them. They'll come down the flume.

378
00:27:09,380 --> 00:27:14,660
They'll expand three and a half times their weight as we clean them.

379
00:27:16,760 --> 00:27:19,460
After the wash water is separated from the chickpeas,

380
00:27:19,820 --> 00:27:22,140
they enter one of two pressure kettles.

381
00:27:22,141 --> 00:27:27,140
Each kettle cooks 1,600 pounds of chickpeas within 15 minutes.

382
00:27:27,141 --> 00:27:31,140
In this area, we grind the peas after they're cooked.

383
00:27:31,141 --> 00:27:34,140
The kettle's dropped into an even feed conveyor.

384
00:27:34,141 --> 00:27:37,140
The even feed conveyor feeds it into a grinder.

385
00:27:37,141 --> 00:27:42,140
The grinder's a lot like a motorboat prop where it takes the peas and throws it up against the screen.

386
00:27:42,141 --> 00:27:46,140
The screen is at the correct aperture to give us a pace and consistency that we're looking for.

387
00:27:47,140 --> 00:27:50,140
Tribe pumps the chickpea paste to the mixing room.

388
00:27:52,140 --> 00:27:56,160
where workers add the rest of the ingredients into the 2,800-pound batch.

389
00:27:57,760 --> 00:28:01,800
Our basic fried hummus recipe consists, again, back to the chickpea,

390
00:28:02,240 --> 00:28:04,480
which can be up to 70% of the final product.

391
00:28:04,920 --> 00:28:09,000
Tahini, which is a ground sesame paste, which is like a peanut butter,

392
00:28:09,280 --> 00:28:11,060
gives it that nutty little flavor to it.

393
00:28:11,360 --> 00:28:13,780
Lemon juice, oil, and then spices.

394
00:28:17,100 --> 00:28:19,700
Once it's mixed, we send it over to the pasteurizer.

395
00:28:19,701 --> 00:28:24,760
The pasteurizer allows us to have a 90-day show of life on all of Tribe's hummus products.

396
00:28:25,660 --> 00:28:34,900
Automated machinery fills, labels, and palletizes each 8-ounce container of hummus.

397
00:28:37,340 --> 00:28:44,000
From 10 million pounds of dried chickpeas a year, Tribe produces nearly 22 million pounds of hummus.

398
00:28:44,001 --> 00:28:51,860
The chickpea was first domesticated over 8,000 years ago in the region known as the Fertile Crescent.

399
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:58,620
It quickly became a staple ingredient among Indian, Middle Eastern, and Mediterranean diets.

400
00:29:00,140 --> 00:29:05,480
Aside from hummus, the chickpea is the main ingredient in another Middle Eastern and Mediterranean dish.

401
00:29:06,860 --> 00:29:10,240
A fried ball of bean paste known as falafel.

402
00:29:10,241 --> 00:29:21,300
Falafel is made out of chickpeas, onion, garlic, parsley, cilantro, cumin, coriander, and spices.

403
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:26,300
Like you see, it's prepared and ground with all these ingredients.

404
00:29:26,980 --> 00:29:30,880
At Alphanous Middle Eastern Cuisine in New York City's Financial District,

405
00:29:32,080 --> 00:29:37,220
owner Mohamed Shami goes through 15 pounds of chickpeas every day for his authentic dishes.

406
00:29:37,221 --> 00:29:43,120
Most of them go into making falafel, whose origins are still a matter of debate.

407
00:29:43,121 --> 00:29:48,520
Some of them, they say that it's made by the Syrians.

408
00:29:48,680 --> 00:29:50,460
Some of them, they say it's by the Palestinians.

409
00:29:50,740 --> 00:29:52,820
And some of them, they say, by the Israelis.

410
00:29:53,440 --> 00:29:56,260
So everybody trying to say that it's...

411
00:29:56,261 --> 00:29:59,240
But all the people in that region, they were all the same thing.

412
00:29:59,340 --> 00:30:02,800
So it came from them. It came from that part of the Middle East.

413
00:30:02,801 --> 00:30:14,513
In 1958, a popular Israeli song, We Have Falafel, included lyrics suggesting instead of kissing the ground to give thanks, new Jewish immigrants got off the plane and

414
00:30:14,514 --> 00:30:15,360
had falafel.

415
00:30:17,240 --> 00:30:25,020
The falafel at Alphanus may not have that stature, but New York Magazine has called it the best in the city for the past several years.

416
00:30:26,020 --> 00:30:30,073
There is a special mold, actually, and this is the authentic mold.

417
00:30:30,074 --> 00:30:32,100
I buy them, actually, from Syria.

418
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:40,220
It's very important to drop each falafel ball in the fryer gently and not on top of each other.

419
00:30:40,900 --> 00:30:43,420
Otherwise, the falafel will fall apart.

420
00:30:44,400 --> 00:30:47,660
Within three minutes, the bean balls become golden brown.

421
00:30:49,740 --> 00:30:51,960
For Mohamed, it's not just the falafel.

422
00:30:52,320 --> 00:30:56,600
It's the way they're presented in the nearly 200 falafel sandwiches he makes every day.

423
00:30:57,340 --> 00:30:59,340
You go to other places, they give you the pocket.

424
00:30:59,341 --> 00:31:02,140
The pocket, you start with the lettuce and the tomato,

425
00:31:02,141 --> 00:31:03,640
and you finish with the falafel.

426
00:31:03,641 --> 00:31:05,340
My sandwich is different.

427
00:31:05,341 --> 00:31:11,340
You start with the falafel, with the salad, with the pickles,

428
00:31:11,341 --> 00:31:13,640
the onion, all of them, and the tahini sauce,

429
00:31:13,641 --> 00:31:16,640
and the hot sauce, if you like, from top to bottom.

430
00:31:16,641 --> 00:31:18,540
I mean, it's not you start with the lettuce,

431
00:31:18,541 --> 00:31:19,540
and you finish with the falafel.

432
00:31:19,541 --> 00:31:20,340
No.

433
00:31:20,341 --> 00:31:25,040
With every bite, you'll taste all of them combined.

434
00:31:25,041 --> 00:31:27,340
I want to make it the right way

435
00:31:27,341 --> 00:31:29,750
way and serve it the authentic way.

436
00:31:29,751 --> 00:31:33,400
I was honest to copy it exactly the way it was served

437
00:31:33,401 --> 00:31:38,300
when I was a kid, back home. While the chickpea may be the most widely

438
00:31:38,301 --> 00:31:43,060
consumed legume in the world, the beans on this counter are among the least consumed.

439
00:31:43,460 --> 00:31:46,600
And it's not just because they cost $35 a pound.

440
00:31:48,980 --> 00:31:53,980
In 2008, an Independence Day celebration in Jerusalem set the record for largest plate

441
00:31:53,981 --> 00:32:00,700
hummus at 881 pounds later that year in london the record was broken with a 924 pound plate

442
00:32:00,701 --> 00:32:09,740
beans will return on modern marvels located in new york city's upper west side is sala maria rossi

443
00:32:09,741 --> 00:32:16,300
an italian restaurant and salumi shop sitting atop the italian meats counter are jars filled

444
00:32:16,301 --> 00:32:20,220
with an assortment of colorful beans you won't find at your local grocery store

445
00:32:20,221 --> 00:32:25,020
In fact, you'd be hard-pressed to find them almost anywhere on Earth.

446
00:32:27,020 --> 00:32:32,100
When I arrived in the U.S., the one thing that was missing me was the beans.

447
00:32:32,860 --> 00:32:36,600
The beans have meat, have fish, have vegetables.

448
00:32:36,920 --> 00:32:39,540
But the beans are not fine exactly what they want.

449
00:32:40,020 --> 00:32:42,140
So I started to import beans.

450
00:32:43,260 --> 00:32:46,080
The import of beans come from various regions in Italy.

451
00:32:46,081 --> 00:32:49,040
but because of their lineage and low yields,

452
00:32:49,440 --> 00:32:52,600
they've earned the distinction of being called heirloom beans.

453
00:32:53,480 --> 00:32:55,860
Heirloom, and in the case of the heirloom beans,

454
00:32:56,080 --> 00:32:59,260
it's like the treasury, the jewelry, the family,

455
00:32:59,440 --> 00:33:01,300
they go generation to generation.

456
00:33:02,120 --> 00:33:05,960
Many of these heirloom beans grow on just a few acres of Italian soil,

457
00:33:06,520 --> 00:33:09,040
but their origins have roots in the Americas.

458
00:33:10,300 --> 00:33:13,580
Most of the beans that they use in Italy,

459
00:33:13,581 --> 00:33:16,220
They come from the U.S. or South America.

460
00:33:16,720 --> 00:33:21,880
When they grow in Italy, the beans, they change characteristics

461
00:33:21,881 --> 00:33:25,980
because from the soil or the macroclime, the way they're growing.

462
00:33:26,780 --> 00:33:29,140
It's not only the environment that changes these beans.

463
00:33:29,800 --> 00:33:32,380
Years of inbreeding have changed them genetically as well.

464
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:35,880
We have the albenghino.

465
00:33:36,120 --> 00:33:39,440
Albenghino, these beans, they are growing near the sea.

466
00:33:39,441 --> 00:33:45,700
The flavor, the sea, the soil more dry.

467
00:33:47,360 --> 00:33:48,740
This one is incredible.

468
